1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,150
We're looking at why making complex systems talk to each other is, well, rarely just a tech issue.

2
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,950
Right. It's fundamentally a human agreement issue.

3
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:14,530
Exactly. Because when systems don't communicate, data becomes fragmented, siloed, and duplicated.

4
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:21,550
Information has to be constantly rekeyed, which leaves systems locked into one supplier and, you know, costly to change.

5
00:00:21,910 --> 00:00:24,610
Yeah, and that's exactly why we need a solid strategy.

6
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:30,910
Right. So today we'll explore what might be the ultimate tool to fix this, the stakeholder map.

7
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:36,450
It really is the ultimate tool. I mean, interoperability is an agreement problem at its core.

8
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:40,990
Uh, like the sources note, "A bus with no members carries nothing."

9
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:44,590
Oh, I love that phrasing. Right. A bus with no members carries nothing.

10
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:49,190
Right. The stakeholder map is that third foundation artifact that, um,

11
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:54,530
it turns a good theoretical design into a reality that actually gets adopted.

12
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:57,790
But wait, that sounds like a massive undertaking.

13
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:03,870
I mean, how do you even know who to put on the bus first without wasting your scarce political capital?

14
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:07,790
Well, you have to sort your agencies into three distinct tiers.

15
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:09,450
Okay, so what's tier one?

16
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:13,390
First you have your champions. They feel the pain of the current system right now.

17
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:16,950
They want the exchange immediately, and they will go first.

18
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:20,070
Got it. So they're highly motivated. And the second tier?

19
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:26,610
Those are your early adopters. They're willing, but, uh, they need to see it actually work before committing their own staff to it.

20
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:29,090
Makes sense. They don't wanna be the guinea pigs.

21
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:32,110
Exactly. And then finally, you have the observers.

22
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:36,190
Let me guess. They are unconvinced and just watching from the sidelines?

23
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:42,790
Yep. Unconvinced, watching closely, and honestly, some are quietly hoping it fails so they don't have to change.

24
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:45,330
Oh, wow. Yeah, I can totally picture that.

25
00:01:45,430 --> 00:01:48,650
But wait, let me push back for a second on the actual data exchange.

26
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:51,030
Every exchange involves two parties, right?

27
00:01:51,570 --> 00:01:57,890
Correct. So why would the side giving the data bother to participate if it just creates extra work and liability for them?

28
00:01:58,470 --> 00:02:01,469
Ah, and that is the exact twist that makes this unique.

29
00:02:02,290 --> 00:02:04,230
Every exchange has a consumer-

30
00:02:04,710 --> 00:02:07,090
The one who wants the data because it makes their service easier...

31
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:09,550
exactly, but it also has a provider,

32
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:15,810
and the provider carries the system load, the cost, and the risk of blame if the data's wrong.

33
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:17,530
Oh, I see.

34
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:23,150
So if a provider sees only cost and no benefit- Adoption stalls... completely.

35
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:24,590
Completely. Right.

36
00:02:25,370 --> 00:02:30,310
So how do we logically sequence this rollout to guarantee success and, you know, build momentum?

37
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:31,770
You have to start small.

38
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:35,890
First, pair a champion consumer with a willing provider.

39
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:36,890
Just those two?

40
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:37,770
Just those two.

41
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:43,550
You use that initial working exchange to prove the once only principle to the early adopters.

42
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:48,990
And the once only principle, that's the idea that someone only has to give their info to the government one time, right?

43
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:54,250
Exactly. So you bring the observers along using hard evidence of a working exchange

44
00:02:55,030 --> 00:02:57,990
rather than, you know, just arguing in strategy meetings.

45
00:02:58,490 --> 00:03:00,410
Because evidence is much harder to ignore.

46
00:03:01,230 --> 00:03:01,410
Right.

47
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:03,630
But here is a crucial step.

48
00:03:04,330 --> 00:03:06,690
You have to lock in the champions in writing.

49
00:03:07,290 --> 00:03:10,370
Wait, really? Like a formal signature. Yes.

50
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:15,930
You host a one-day workshop ending with a signed commitment letter and a named focal point.

51
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:19,650
Programs that skip the signature, they just stall quickly.

52
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:22,090
Wow, so you need that real accountability.

53
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:23,050
Absolutely.

54
00:03:23,690 --> 00:03:28,930
And finally, you establish a governing body to own the framework, admit members, and resolve disputes.

55
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:30,730
Because without an owner...

56
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:33,630
The platform will decay in its second year.

57
00:03:33,890 --> 00:03:34,090
Right.

58
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:40,230
So the big takeaway here is that stakeholder mapping deliberately solves the agreement problem.

59
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:46,470
It shows you exactly who to onboard first, who to convince with evidence, and where you need leadership to lean in.

60
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:50,050
It really is the blueprint for actually getting things done.

